{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Fix pushed — Gerrit change 1643\n\nhttps://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1643 — \"Bound op-cert service lifespan shutdown\"\n\n### What changed\n\n- `src/rebar/opcert_service/config.py` — new `shutdown_cancel_seconds`\n  (`REBAR_OPCERT_SHUTDOWN_CANCEL_SECONDS`, default 5, falling back on a missing / unparseable /\n  non-positive value), mirroring `review_bot.config.shutdown_cancel_seconds`.\n- `src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` — the worker now offloads through a module-level `_offload`\n  seam onto an app-OWNED `ThreadPoolExecutor` created in `lifespan`, instead of\n  `asyncio.to_thread`. Shutdown cancels the tasks, joins them under `shutdown_cancel_seconds`,\n  then ABANDONS the executor with `shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True)`.\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` — the two worker tests move from patching\n  `asyncio.to_thread` to the `_offload` seam; new regression test\n  `test_shutdown_does_not_wait_for_an_in_flight_job`.\n\nKeeping the job OFF the loop's default executor is the load-bearing part. Bounding the task await\nalone does NOT fix this: the block lives in `loop.shutdown_default_executor()`, which joins the\norphaned thread with no timeout, and that runs at loop teardown regardless of how promptly the\ntask cancelled.\n\n### Verification\n\nRegression test is genuinely RED then GREEN — I reverted ONLY `app.py` (keeping the new config\nfield so the test could still construct its config) and ran it against the pre-fix module:\n\n    FAILED test_shutdown_does_not_wait_for_an_in_flight_job\n    AssertionError: shutdown waited 30.0s for the in-flight job\n    assert 30.008877125103027 < 15.0\n\nAgainst the fixed module the same test passes and the whole module runs in ~1s.\n\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` — 9 passed.\n- 10 consecutive runs of that module under `-n 4 --dist worksteal --timeout=60\n  --timeout-method=thread` on py3.11.13 — 10/10 green, 1.0-2.0s each (AC: stability).\n- `pytest tests/unit -k \"opcert or review_bot or shutdown\"` under `-n 4 --dist worksteal` — 335\n  passed.\n- `make lint` exit 0; `make typecheck` — success, 504 source files.\n\n`test_matching_guard_is_accepted` is untouched: same request, same `assert resp.status_code == 202`.\nNot skipped, not quarantined, not retried.\n\n### Caveat carried forward, not hidden\n\nAs recorded in the previous comment, what makes an in-flight job slow enough to reach the 300s CI\nbudget is still INFERRED rather than demonstrated — the faked `run_job` in this test is instant, and\nnaive repeats never reproduced. This change removes the only unbounded wait reachable from the test,\nwhich makes the stall impossible regardless of the cause of slowness, but it does not by itself\nprove what that cause was. If the signature recurs after this lands, that inference is wrong and the\nticket should be reopened rather than treated as fixed.\n\nAwaiting `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786518506957307001,"uuid":"4f2e30cc-d4fa-4ffe-9e93-90a784230167"}